Since Dylan seems to be off sick, I thought I'd do some source diving
to answer this question.  Disclaimer: I have not checked the following
information in wizard mode, and most of it covers things I would not
do in a normal game of Nethack.  Dylan, if you're reading this, please
correct any mistakes in this post.

Apart from "bad" prayer or sacrificing, the only thing that can anger
your god is displacing your pet in such a way as to kill it ("You feel
guilty about losing your pet like this.")  Note that just plain
killing your pet ("You hear the rumble of distant thunder") does not
anger your god, although it loses you 15 alignment and 1 Luck.

> Praying before your prayer time out is over

Yes.

> Praying with negative luck
> Praying with negative alignment

It appears from pray.c that these will cause a negative effect like
those of praying too soon (possibly feeling that your god is
displeased, possibly something worse) and will strip away your
protection, but will not actually anger your god in a way that
requires appeasement.

> Killing a co-aligned priest

No, although as well as being murder it loses you protection.

> Killing a co-aligned unicorn

No, "only" -5 Luck.

> Sacrificing a co-aligned unicorn

On a co-aligned altar, yes.  On a cross-aligned altar, you convert
yourself instead, but that doesn't anger your god.  Sacrificing a
cross-aligned unicorn on an altar of its own alignment (e.g. a neutral
character sacrificing a white unicorn on a lawful altar) is bad but
doesn't seem to anger your god (only the altar's god?).

> Sacrificing a former pet

This looks like another case of angering the altar's god.

> Sacrificing your own race as a non chaotic

This, however, always severely angers your own god (three points of
anger).

> Praying on a cross aligned altar

Only when making unholy water.

> Engraving on a co-aligned altar

No, you only lose 1 Wis.

> Praying in Gehennom

It appears that this simply calls angrygods(u.ualign.type), which
causes lost protection plus the negative effects mentioned earlier,
but does not anger your god.

> Sacrificing on an unaligned altar in Gehennom

Yes (by three points again).

Also, you missed knowingly sacrificing a fake Amulet of Yendor, and
sacrificing on a cross-aligned altar with negative alignment and
having already converted yourself - this will cause your sacrifice to
be rejected just like sacrificing on an unaligned altar in Gehennom.
